--- a/docs/PROFILE_GUIDE.md
+++ b/docs/PROFILE_GUIDE.md
@@ -89,7 +89,7 @@ For a picture of the port's own beside one of the game's screens, such as a hint
 
 `edited_textures` lists textures the profile draws again as they load, with `edit_texture` doing the drawing: the game's button symbols in the style of the gamepad in use, for one. Each is an `EditedTexture`, named and found as a `SharpTexture` is. `edit_texture` gets the game's decoded picture (RGBA8) and a `TextureEditContext`, and returns its own picture, the same shape at any size (four times gives room for sharp detail), drawn with the same texture coordinates as a texture pack's image; false keeps the game's. The context holds what the picture may depend on: Button prompts with the gamepad in use and the Confirm button setting (`input::ButtonPrompts`: the style, which face button sends each of the PSP's, and whether L and R are on the triggers, which the Controls page's Swap shoulders and triggers does for every game), and whether Texture filter is Sharp. When it changes, the cached textures are dropped and the listed ones drawn again. Set `button_prompts` when they are the game's button symbols, and the Controls page offers Button prompts (Automatic, PSP, Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo, Keyboard; Automatic follows player 1's pad, or the keyboard when that was played last or no pad is connected, and the PSP's on a phone without one). In the keyboard style `ButtonPrompts::keys` holds the first key or mouse button bound to each of the PSP's buttons (`input::PromptKey`), so the picture can show it; it is empty in the other styles. A texture pack's image for one is drawn instead. `<prefix>_NO_TEXTURE_EDITS=1` draws the game's own, for a comparison. Keep the game's pixels out of the profile: draw over the picture it is given.
 
-`cheats` lists cheats the port offers on the menu's Cheats page, beside the player's own PPSSPP cheat file (`PSP/Cheats/<disc id>.ini` on the memory stick, `host/cheats/cheats.hpp`). Each is a `BuiltinCheat`: a key for settings.ini, the heading it goes under, a name, a sentence for the footer, and its choices, each a label and its CWCheat code lines (what a cheat file has after `_L`). One choice makes a switch; several make Off and each. They run through the same engine as the file, before it, every 77 ms of the game's time, so a code written as a player would write it gives the same result. A write into recompiled code is left out, as for the file; such a code needs the interpreter, which cheats do not reach yet. Test each against the game's own screens before listing it: a public list's codes can name an address the game no longer reads. Without any, the page has only the switch and the file.
+`cheats` lists cheats the port offers on the menu's Cheats page, beside the player's own PPSSPP cheat file (`PSP/Cheats/<disc id>.ini` on the memory stick, `host/cheats/cheats.hpp`). Each is a `BuiltinCheat`: a key for settings.ini, the heading it goes under, a name, a sentence for the footer, and its choices, each a label and its CWCheat code lines (what a cheat file has after `_L`). One choice makes a switch; several make Off and each. They run through the same engine as the file, before it, every 77 ms of the game's time, so a code written as a player would write it gives the same result. A write into recompiled code is left out, as for the file; such a code needs the interpreter, which cheats do not reach yet. Test each against the game's own screens before listing it: a public list's codes can name an address the game no longer reads. Without any, the page has only the switch and the file. No cheat runs while the game has ad hoc play running (`cheats::paused_online()`): it would change one player's game only, so a port's own cheat that runs from a hook rather than as codes asks the same.
 
 `interpolation_thresholds` is for frame interpolation, which blends the game's frames and must not blend across a camera cut. The angles and fractions it uses to tell a cut from motion hold for any game; its distances are in the game's own world units, and the defaults were measured on Monster Hunter Portable 3rd. A game whose world is on a very different scale gives its own; one that does not, gets those defaults.
 
diff --git a/host/cheats/cheats.cpp b/host/cheats/cheats.cpp
index 9e4931f..351801a 100644
--- a/host/cheats/cheats.cpp
+++ b/host/cheats/cheats.cpp
@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@ struct State {
     std::vector<bool> builtin_changes_code;
     std::uint64_t interval_us{kDefaultIntervalUs};
     std::uint64_t next_us{};
+    bool paused{};  // the last pass found ad hoc play running (for the log)
 };
 
 State &state() {
@@ -167,6 +168,12 @@ void on_vblank() {
     if (now < s.next_us) return;
     s.next_us = now + s.interval_us;
     if (!settings::current().cheats) return;
+    if (const bool paused = paused_online(); paused != s.paused) {
+        s.paused = paused;
+        std::cout << (paused ? "[cheats] paused: the game is playing ad hoc\n"
+                             : "[cheats] running again: the game has left ad hoc play\n");
+    }
+    if (s.paused) return;
     const std::lock_guard<std::mutex> hold(s.lock);
     if (!s.loaded) load(s);
     if (s.runtime == nullptr) return;
@@ -261,6 +268,11 @@ Overview overview() {
     return o;
 }
 
+bool paused_online() {
+    static const bool allowed = env("CHEATS_ONLINE") != nullptr;
+    return !allowed && adhoc_networking_on();
+}
+
 void set_enabled(std::size_t index, bool enabled) {
     State &s = state();
     const std::lock_guard<std::mutex> hold(s.lock);
